UID:c5d72888ca2b0024@role-civil-litigation-appellate.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261123T172614Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261119
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] US Department of Labor announces final rule c
 larifying employee\, independent contractor status under federal wage and 
 hour laws
DESCRIPTION:WASHINGTON – The U.S. Department of Labor’s Wage and Hour
  Division today announced a final rule to revise its analysis for disting
 uishing between employees and independent contractors under the Fair Labor
  Standards Act and two related federal laws. The new final rule provides c
 larity to businesses and workers alike\, as well as the division’s enfor
 cement personnel.U.S. Secretary of Labor Keith Sonderling\, Wage and Hour 
 Division Administrator Andrew Rogers\, and other department officials host
 ed an event to formally announce the final rule at the Frances Perkins Bui
 lding this morning. Employers\, business associations\, worker advocates\,
  independent contractors\, and other stakeholders were in attendance as Se
 cretary Sonderling highlighted the clarity and simplicity of the new regul
 ations.The final rule rescinds an earlier 2024 rule addressing the classif
 ication of employees and independent contractors and replaces it with an a
 nalysis that is substantially similar to the framework adopted by the depa
 rtment in 2021. Consistent with the outcomes of federal appellate cases\, 
 the final rule provides further guidance on the process for differentiatin
 g between employees with protections under the FLSA and individuals who wo
 rk as independent contractors.“It’s more important than ever to ensure
  our labor regulations keep pace with the changing ways that Americans cho
 ose to work\, and businesses choose to operate\,” said Secretary Sonderl
 ing. “The new Independent Contractor final rule establishes clear\, pred
 ictable\, and easy-to-understand criteria for determining who is an employ
 ee and who is an independent contractor – allowing millions of Americans
  to take advantage of new opportunities in President Trump’s booming eco
 nomy.”The final rule also applies the department’s streamlined analysi
 s to the Family and Medical Leave Act\, and the Migrant and Seasonal Agric
 ultural Worker Protection Act\, both of which use the FLSA’s statutory d
 efinition of “employ.” However\, the rule does not chan\nhttp://www.do
 l.gov/newsroom/releases/whd/whd20261119
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/whd/whd20261119
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:4b75710f6119c9c4@role-civil-litigation-appellate.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261123T172614Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261118
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] US Department of Labor obtains court order re
 quiring NJ-based  construction contractor to pay $2.2M in back wages\, dam
 ages\, penalties
DESCRIPTION:ENGLEWOOD CLIFFS\, NJ – The U.S. Department of Labor has obt
 ained a consent judgment requiring a construction contractor to pay more t
 han $2 million in back wages and damages to 323 construction workers.  
    Entered Nov. 16\, 2026\, in the U.S. District Court for the District
  of New Jersey\, the consent judgment resolves the department’s litigati
 on alleging Premier Concrete Systems LLC and its owners\, Peter Tiflinsky 
 and Antonio Escada\, violated overtime and recordkeeping provisions of the
  Fair Labor Standards Act.The litigation was a result of an investigation 
 by the department’s Wage and Hour Division that found the company and it
 s owners paid employees cash at straight-time rates for all hours worked a
 nd failed to pay them time and one-half their regular rate of pay for hour
 s over 40 in a workweek as required by the FLSA. “Construction workers 
 play a critical role across the country in ensuring safe and reliable stru
 ctures. The Wage and Hour Division is committed to ensuring that construct
 ion workers are paid in compliance with federal labor laws\,” said Wage 
 and Hour Division Regional Administrator James Cain in Philadelphia.The ju
 dgment requires Premier Concrete Systems to pay 323 employees back wages t
 otaling $1\,040\,648 and an equal amount in damages. The judgment also req
 uires the defendants to pay a civil penalty of $118\,704 and prohibits def
 endants from violating the FLSA in the future.“The resolution of this ca
 se is a clear reminder for employers in the construction industry to remem
 ber their responsibilities under the law\,” said Regional Solicitor of L
 abor Jeffrey S. Rogoff in New York City.Employees and employers can contac
 t the division with questions and requests for compliance assistance at it
 s toll-free helpline\, 866-4US-WAGE (487-9243). Employers are encouraged t
 o use the agency’s industry-specific compliance assistance toolkits to l
 earn about their responsibilities under the laws enforced by the division.
 The agency’s PAID program offers employers an opp\nhttp://www.dol.gov/ne
 wsroom/releases/whd/whd20261118
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/whd/whd20261118
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:a8cf8239219c196a@role-civil-litigation-appellate.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261123T172614Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261117
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] US Department of Labor issues opinion letters
  addressing Fair Labor Standards Act exemption\, Family and Medical Leave 
 Act hours of service
DESCRIPTION:WASHINGTON – The U.S. Department of Labor’s Wage and Hour 
 Division today issued two opinion letters that address real-world question
 s related to the Fair Labor Standards Act and the Family and Medical Leave
  Act.The two opinion letters address the applicability of the exemption fo
 r amusement and recreational establishments under the Fair Labor Standard
 s Act and the hours-of-service requirement under the Family and Medical L
 eave Act. Opinion letters provide official written interpretations from t
 he department’s Wage and Hour Division that explain how the laws that t
 he department enforces apply to specific factual circumstances and may hel
 p the public understand their rights and responsibilities. The division is
  committed to providing opinion letters that help American workers and bus
 inesses understand how the FLSA and FMLA apply to their situations.   Th
 e two opinion letters issued today are:FLSA2026-14: Whether the exemption
  for seasonal amusement and recreational establishments in section 13(a)(3
 ) of the FLSA applies to a concessionaire that provides food and drinks at
  events such as a state fair and concerts.FMLA2026-3: Whether time spent w
 riting mandatory reports outside of scheduled work hours and traveling bet
 ween worksites counts as hours of service under the FMLA. In 2025\, the d
 epartment announced the relaunch of the opinion letter program\, which ex
 pands its longstanding commitment to providing meaningful compliance assis
 tance that helps workers\, employers\, and other stakeholders understand h
 ow federal labor laws apply in specific workplace circumstances.Visit the 
 Wage and Hour Division’s opinion letters page to explore past guidance
  and find information on how to submit a request for an opinion letter.Emp
 loyees and employers can contact the Wage and Hour Division with questions
  and requests for compliance assistance at its toll-free helpline\, 866-4
 US-WAGE (487-9243). Employers are encouraged to use the agency’s industr
 y-specific compliance assistance toolkits\nhttp://www.dol.gov/newsroom/re
 leases/whd/whd20261117
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/whd/whd20261117
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:a4edf065f9b1c458@role-civil-litigation-appellate.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261123T172614Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261116
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] US Department of Labor applauds federal appea
 ls court decision on administrative exhaustion requirement under federal b
 enefits law
DESCRIPTION:WASHINGTON – The U.S. Department of Labor applauded a federa
 l appeals court decision allowing employees to bring legal claims directly
  to federal court if they reasonably suspect fiduciary violations in their
  employee benefit plans covered under the Employee Retirement Income Secur
 ity Act.Issued Oct. 13 by the en banc 11th U.S. Circuit Court of Appeals\,
  the unanimous decision in Bolton v. Inland Fresh Seafood overturns preced
 ent that required employees to exhaust all administrative remedies under t
 heir ERISA-covered employee benefit plans before they could go to federal 
 court. “This decision vindicates the department’s reinvigorated amicu
 s program and its focus on statutory text\,” said Solicitor of Labor Jon
 athan Berry. “ERISA neither explicitly nor implicitly requires hard-work
 ing Americans to go anywhere but federal court to vindicate their statutor
 y rights under it. By overruling its precedent that erroneously held other
 wise\, the 11th Circuit joins all but one of its sister circuits and reinf
 orces ERISA’s uniform statutory framework.”The court held that the exh
 austion requirement was not based on ERISA’s text\, saying it was an “
 atextual product of judicial creation based solely on policy reasons.” T
 he court’s opinion also quoted the department’s oral argument\, saying
  “plaintiffs or employers should not be able to contract away or force t
 heir employees to contract away their rights under ERISA.” “The decis
 ion by the 11th Circuit rightly rejects a judicially invented exhaustion 
 requirement that has no basis in ERISA’s text or Congressional intent\,
 ” said Assistant Secretary of Labor for the Employee Benefits Security A
 dministration Daniel Aronowitz. “By aligning with the law’s clear text
  and Congress's intent to provide participants and beneficiaries direct ac
 cess to federal court\, the court’s decision makes certain that ERISA’
 s protections are applied as written in the statute itself.”Employers an
 d workers can contact EBSA at askebsa.dol.gov or call 866-444-3272 to\nht
 tp://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/ebsa/ebsa20261116
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/ebsa/ebsa20261116
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
